WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.152
Runbook Press begins with a familiar mess: maps in one folder, session notes in another, NPC motives in chat, rulings in memory, and a campaign that deserves more than another scattered handoff.

2
00:00:08.152 --> 00:00:16.303
The point is not to make a generic book. The point is to turn approved source material into a useful artifact.

3
00:00:16.303 --> 00:00:24.455
A player primer that welcomes someone new. A district guide that makes a place easy to run.

4
00:00:24.455 --> 00:00:32.606
A mission packet that carries the right details without exposing the wrong ones.

5
00:00:32.606 --> 00:00:40.758
A season book that lets the table keep what it built. Runbook Press keeps the split clear. Player-safe pages stay readable.

6
00:00:40.758 --> 00:00:48.909
GM-only material stays behind the curtain.

7
00:00:48.909 --> 00:00:57.061
Credits, changes, source notes, and approval state remain attached, so a polished page does not become a loose claim.

8
00:00:57.061 --> 00:01:05.212
The workflow should feel like a small editorial room inside Chummer: draft, arrange, review, format, export, and revise without losing provenance.

9
00:01:05.212 --> 00:01:13.364
Layout matters because people use books under pressure.

10
00:01:13.364 --> 00:01:21.515
They need the map, the faction note, the timeline, or the handout fast enough that the table keeps moving.

11
00:01:21.515 --> 00:01:29.667
Runbook Press is campaign publishing with memory, structure, and restraint.
